  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nConditions through the later half of the workweek will be \ndefined by the redeveloping upper level ridge over the western \nand central CONUS. For today, cyclonic flow is found across the \nnorthern Plains as a stacked upper level low begins to move over\nHudson Bay. Seasonably cool highs in the lower 70s north to \nlower 80s southwest can thus be anticipated across the forecast \narea this afternoon. Mainly dry conditions are expected today, \nthough a handful of short term CAMs continue to advertise low \n(<20 percent) potential for some showers across portions of the\nfar southwest later this afternoon into this evening as a \nshallow shortwave perturbation ejects off the Hudson bay low. \nInterrogation of BUFKIT model soundings during this period does \nreveal at least moderate instability (1000 - 1250 J/KG of model \nMUCAPE) and decent bulk shear values (30 to 40 knots) developing\nover the southwest late this afternoon, which could allow some \nisolated rumbles of thunder. The elevated nature of these \nstorms will likely keep these storms sub severe in nature, \nthough very dry near surface conditions, modest DCAPE values \napproaching 1000 J/KG, and sufficient 1-3km shear values around\n35 knots may allow for gusty winds when and where any storms do\ndevelop. The overall potential for severe weather remains very \nlow. \n\nLate tonight into Wednesday, a redeveloping upper level ridge \nover the western CONUS will subject the northern Plains to \nnorthwesterly flow. With the passage of another, slightly more \nrobust, shortwave sliding along this northwesterly flow, chances\nfor showers and potential some thunderstorms increase (10 to 20\npercent) from west to east through Wednesday morning and \nafternoon. With surface flow turning south to southeasterly \nallowing for greater moisture flux into the southcentral and \nsouth east Wednesday afternoon during peak heating, an uptick in\nshower and storm coverage can be anticipated. The overall \nconvective environment appears to be fairly modest, with MUCAPE \nvalues approaching 1000 J/KG and fairly poor bulk shear values \ninitially around 25 knots or less. Through the late afternoon \nand evening, increasing bulk shear values (up to around 30 to 40\nknots across the south central into the south east) will brief \nbe of a stronger to marginally severe storm or two. The main \nhazards associated with any severe storm would be large hail up \nto the size of quarters, and damaging wind up to 60 MPH. SPC has\na small portion of south central North Dakota under a level 1 \nof 5 risk for severe weather on Wednesday. Diminishing MUCAPE \nand increasing CIN through the mid to late evening is then \nexpected to limit the storm potential through the remainder of \nthe night. Otherwise for Wednesday, the building upper level \nridge moving into our west will allow for the greater \npenetration of the thermal axis into the northern Plains, \npromoting near normal high temperatures in the lower to upper \n80s. \n\nLooking ahead into the remainder of the week, near to slightly\nabove normal and mainly dry conditions are anticipated as the \nupper level ridge becomes more situated over the Great Plains. \nSome cooler highs in the upper 70s are possible across the north\nFriday afternoon as a weak impulse slides down the eastern \nextent the ridge, but otherwise highs in the 80s are expected \nthrough at least Saturday. The ensemble becomes increasingly \ndiscordant late Saturday into Sunday as it attempts to resolve \nthe evolution of a more robust pacific trough / low cutting \nacross the southern Canadian Prairies. A slight majority (55 \npercent) of ensemble members favor a rapid flattening of the \nridge late Saturday into Sunday, allowing for low to medium \nchances (20 to 40 percent) for precpitation and somewhat cooler \nhighs in the upper 70s to mid 80s on Sunday. A minority cluster \n(45 percent) of members instead favor a slower evolution of this\npattern, allowing for a brief amplification of the ridge over \nthe northern Plains heading into Sunday, before rapidly \nflattening late Sunday into early Monday. This pattern would \ndelay the onset of precpitation until later in the day Sunday, \nand would allow highs mainly in the 80s to persist through the \nweekend. In either scenario, the ensemble broadly shows a return\nto warmer and drier weather heading into the early half of next\nweek as a redeveloping ridge pattern takes hold of the central \nCONUS.",
